{"name":"Undine","hex":"#89c1ba","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Undine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from Latin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Undine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Undine","blocks":[{"text":"Undines are a category of elemental beings associated with water, stemming from the alchemical writings of Paracelsus. Later writers developed the undine into a water nymph in its own right, and it continues to live in modern literature and art through such adaptations as Friedrich de la Motte Fouqué's Undine (1811) and Hans Christian Andersen's \"The Little Mermaid\" (1837)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Baden_Undine_Kurpark.jpg/330px-Baden_Undine_Kurpark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undine","wikidata":"Q192032"},{"title":"Undine (novella)","blocks":[{"text":"Undine is a fairytale novella by Friedrich de la Motte Fouqué in which Undine, a water spirit, marries a knight named Huldbrand in order to gain a soul. Published in 1811, it is an early German romance, which has been translated into English and other languages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Undine_%28novella%29_-_cover_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_18752.jpg/330px-Undine_%28novella%29_-_cover_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_18752.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undine_(novella)","wikidata":"Q664813"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Les_Ondines_-_Antoine_Calbet.jpg/500px-Les_Ondines_-_Antoine_Calbet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Les_Ondines_-_Antoine_Calbet.jpg/1280px-Les_Ondines_-_Antoine_Calbet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c